  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1...Warmer today with increasing humidity. \nScattered showers and a few thunderstorms are expected across \nwestern NY.\n\nGOES water vapor imagery shows increasing moisture across the \nregion as well as a few disturbances approaching from the south \nand west this afternoon. Cumulus clouds are rapidly developing \nin an area of instability across western NY. A south-southwest \nflow will continue to advect warm, moist air with PWATS climbing\nto near 1.5\" by this evening across western NY. The combination\nof approaching large scale ascent and deep moisture, and \ndaytime heating will support scattered showers and few \nthunderstorms across western NY through early evening. The flow \naloft is weak and therefore the threat for severe thunderstorms \nis low, however localized heavy rain is possible through this \nevening. Drier air will continue to reside across the eastern \nLake Ontario region this afternoon. \n\nCoverage of showers and thunderstorms will lessen across western NY \ntonight, however chances for showers and an isolated \nthunderstorm will increase across the Saint Lawrence Valley. \n  \nKey Message 2...The chance for showers and thunderstorms increases \nThursday and Friday, with unsettled weather continuing through the \nweekend. Any thunderstorms may produce locally heavy downpours.  \n\nA deep southwest flow will remain across the region ahead of an \napproaching shortwave trough Thursday. Deep moisture remains across \nthe region, with the axis of the highest PWATS (2\") near the east \ncoast. Diurnally driven showers and thunderstorms are expected to \ndevelop inland from the Lakes with locally heavy rainfall possible. \nWhile 0-6km shear is expected to increase to 25kts Thursday \nafternoon, mid-level lapse rates remain weak. The severe threat \nremains low. \n\nAnother shortwave trough and approaching front are expected to \napproach the region Friday. Uncertainty in coverage and timing of \nshowers and thunderstorms increases Friday. Cloud cover as well as \nshowers in the morning could limit how much instability forms\nthrough peak heating. If clearing occurs, another round of \ndiurnally driven showers and a few thunderstorms are possible \nwith greater chances inland from the Lakes. The muggy airmass \nwill remain across the region and locally heavy rainfall can't \nbe ruled out.\n\nThe weather will remain unsettled through the weekend as a trough \nflattens out across the forecast area. A cold front is expected to \nmove across the region which will be a focus of showers and \nthunderstorms Saturday. Another front is expected to approach the \nregion Sunday with diurnally driven showers and thunderstorms. While \nit doesn't look like a washout, daily chances of showers and\nthunderstorms are expected.",
